1) Starting
-----------

Open the Diversions directory and double-click !Lander2. It takes over
the whole screen (or opens in a window, if you chose that last time)
and shows its title page. Its icon also appears on the right of the
icon bar, and stays there while you are back at the desktop.

On the title page, move up and down with the arrow keys (or point with
the mouse) and press Return (or click) to choose:

  Play Invasion      the new game
  Play Lander+       the 1987 game
  Settings           the look, sound, controls and so on (section 6)
  High scores        the best games so far
  How to play        four pages on flying, the keys and the aliens
  View source        read the program in !JsEdit (section 8)
  Original Lander    run !Lander, the faithful copy of the original
  Back to desktop    leave the game; its icon stays on the icon bar

Escape on the title page also goes back to the desktop. If you leave
the title page alone for 25 seconds, a demonstration game starts,
flown by the computer: press a key or click to stop it.

Click SELECT on the icon bar icon to come back to the game. Click MENU
on it for:

  Info                  about Lander II
  Full screen           play on the whole desktop screen
  Window                play in a desktop window, which you can move
                        and resize
  Browser full screen   use the whole of your computer's screen, not
                        just the web page (the F key does the same)
  View source           open the program in !JsEdit
  Open directory        open the application directory in the Filer
  Quit                  stop the game and take the icon away

In a window, the game only hears the keys while the window has the
input focus: click in it first. If another window takes the input
focus during a game, the game pauses. Clicking the window's close icon
goes back to the desktop, as Escape on the title page does.


2) The two games
----------------

Invasion

  Aliens arrive in waves and spray a red virus over the land. Shoot
  them before it spreads. Infected land turns red on the landscape
  and reddish brown on the scanner, and trees the virus reaches may
  mutate (they turn red and shed virus of their own: shoot them too,
  for 40 points).

  When every alien in a wave is destroyed, a tally shows how you did,
  and the clean land scores a bonus: the percentage of land still
  clean, times 10, times the wave number, and 2000 more if there is
  no virus left at all. Then the land is cleaned and the next wave
  comes. Waves 5 and 10 bring a new landscape; gravity gets stronger
  as the waves go on.

  The aliens, and what they score:

    Seeder     100   a blue saucer that drops virus as it flies, then
                     lands and sprays everything round it (50 if you
                     get it on the ground)
    Drone      300   patrols and shoots at you; if it finds a mutated
                     tree it becomes a mutant
    Mutant     500   hunts you down, firing accurately
    Pest       400   small and fast, it always homes in on you
    Bomber     800   high, fast and straight, dropping bombs that
                     burst into virus; takes two hits
    Fighter    900   fires in bursts; takes two hits (200 for the
                     first, 700 for the second)
    Attractor 1000   rare; hovers high, strikes the land with virus
                     and pulls you in with a beam that drains your
                     fuel; takes six hits

  From wave 6 the aliens also fire homing missiles. There is a
  harmless sea monster in the sea on some landscapes.

  Every shot you fire costs a point. Every 5000 points you get an
  extra ship, missile and smart bomb. The game ends when you run out
  of ships or (at Normal and Hard difficulty) when 90% of the land has
  been infected for ten seconds: "THE LAND IS LOST".

Lander+

  The original game, on the original 256 x 256 tile landscape: you
  start with 500 points and three ships, score 20 for each tree or
  building you shoot and lose a point for each shot. Once you pass 800
  points, rocks start falling from the sky, and gravity gets stronger
  at 1024 and 1488 points. There are no aliens: the challenge is the
  flying.

When a game ends with a score good enough for the table, type your
name (up to 10 letters) and press Return. The name you typed last
time is offered: press Return to keep it, or just type a new one.


3) Flying
---------

Lander is flown in an unusual way, and it takes a little practice.

The mouse doesn't steer the ship left and right: it sets which way the
ship leans. Imagine the pointer sitting in the middle of the screen.
There, the ship is level, and its engine pushes straight up.
Move the mouse away from the middle and the ship leans over in that
direction: the further you move it, the more it leans, until a long
way out it turns right over. Move the mouse back towards the middle
and the ship comes level again. The ship doesn't snap into place: it
swings smoothly towards the angle the mouse asks for.

The engine always pushes the way the top of the ship faces, so a
leaning ship is pushed sideways as well as up. To fly somewhere, lean
a little that way and give short bursts of thrust; to stop, lean the
other way.
Gravity pulls you down all the time, and touching the ground anywhere
but the launch pad is a crash (so is flying into a tree or building).

While you play, the pointer is hidden and held in the game window
(click once if the mouse doesn't seem to do anything). A small
cross at the bottom right of the screen shows where the invisible
mouse is. If you lose track, press C: the mouse goes back to the
middle and the ship levels out. The same happens at the start of each
game and each new ship.

  Mouse           Keys               What it does
  Select (left)   Space or Z         thrust (full power)
  Menu (middle)   X or Shift         hover (gentle, fuel-saving thrust)
  Adjust (right)  Return or Ctrl     fire (hold it down)
  move            Up / Down (W / S)  lean more / lean less
  move            Left / Right       turn the direction the ship
                  (A / D)            leans
                  M                  fire a homing missile
                  B                  set off a smart bomb
                  C                  put the mouse back in the middle
                  P                  pause
                  Escape             pause (in a game), back (menus)

The keys always work, with or without the mouse. With a gamepad, the
left stick leans the ship (let go and it levels out), A (or the right
trigger) thrusts, X (or the right shoulder button) fires, B (or the
left trigger) hovers, Y fires a missile, the left shoulder button sets
off a smart bomb, Back re-centres and Start pauses. In the menus the
pad's arrows move, A chooses and B goes back.

The pause menu (P or Escape) offers Resume, Settings, Restart and Quit
to title. The game also pauses if you switch to another program or
browser tab.


4) Fuel, landing and weapons
----------------------------

Fuel. Thrust uses fuel quickly and hover slowly (and in Lander+, so
does firing, as in the original). The fuel gauge flashes and "LOW
FUEL" appears when it is nearly empty; with none left the engine
won't work.

Landing. The launch pad is the flat grey square where you start. Come
down on it gently, nearly level, and you land and refuel. Too fast and
you crash (in Lander+) or bounce off (in Invasion, where the pad is
kinder). In Invasion, while you sit on the pad:

  - you are safe from the aliens' fire;
  - your missiles are topped up, one every three seconds, to the
    number you started with (not at Zarch difficulty);
  - if you have shot any aliens since you last landed, you get a
    landing bonus of 50 points times the wave number.

Your shield. In Invasion your fuel is also your shield: each hit from
an alien's bullet, bomb or missile costs fuel, and the screen flashes
red. With an empty tank the next hit destroys the ship. Flying into
an alien destroys you both.

Missiles (M) home in on the nearest alien in front of you (or the
nearest anywhere). You can carry up to nine.

Smart bombs (B) destroy every alien in and around the view, and every
bullet, bomb and speck of virus there. Attractors only lose half
their strength. You can carry up to three.

Pickups. A mutant or fighter sometimes leaves a spinning pyramid
behind, floating over the land. Fly into it to collect it before it
fades away (15 seconds):

  Fuel       1000 units of fuel
  Missile    one more missile
  Shield     the next three hits cost nothing
  Cleanse    cleans the virus off the land round it

After you lose a ship, the next one starts on the pad, safe from harm
for a few seconds.


5) The scanner and the gauges
-----------------------------

In the Enhanced look a panel across the top of the screen shows:

  The scanner, at the top left: a map with one dot for each tile. Land
  is green, sea blue, the virus reddish brown and the launch pad grey.
  You are the flashing white dot; aliens are coloured blips (seeders
  and bombers cyan, drones orange, mutants orange and red, pests
  magenta, fighters yellow, attractors red) and pickups flash white.
  The outline shows the part of the land you can see. You stay in the
  middle of the scanner, a little below half way, and the map scrolls
  round as you fly, as in Defender. In Invasion that is the whole
  world (it is 64 tiles across, and wraps round); in Lander+ it is
  the land round you.

  SCORE and HI (the best score in the table, or yours if it is
  better).

  A ship for each spare ship, then (in Invasion) a yellow mark for each
  missile and a red one for each smart bomb; the wave number and how
  much of the land the VIRUS has covered (green, then orange, then
  flashing red). In Lander+ this line says FLYING, LANDED,
  REFUELLING or CRASHED.

  FUEL, in orange (flashing red when low), and ALT, your height above
  the launch pad, in green.

Messages such as "WAVE 2", "BASE UNDER ATTACK" (virus near the pad) and
"INFECTION 75%" appear in the middle of the screen.

The Classic look has Lander's own score bar instead: the score at the
top left, the number of ships and the high score on the second line,
and the fuel as an orange line just below. In Invasion the second line
also shows W (wave), V (virus), M (missiles) and B (smart bombs).


6) Settings
-----------

Choose Settings on the title page or the pause menu. Move to a setting
with Up and Down, and change it with Left and Right (or click SELECT
on it for the next value and ADJUST for the one before). A line at the
bottom says what each one does. The settings marked (Enhanced) below
are grey in the Classic look, and Arcade voices is grey unless the
Sound is Arcade. They are saved when you choose Back.

  Look            Enhanced (the new look), or Classic 1987: 320 x 256
                  pixels, 12 x 10 tiles of landscape and the
                  Archimedes' 256 colours, as Lander drew it
  View distance   (Enhanced) how much landscape you see: Near (as
                  Lander), Medium, Far or Huge
  Resolution      (Enhanced) how many lines the picture has: 640 x 512,
                  960 x 768 or Native (your screen's own); the width
                  fits the shape of the screen. Lower is faster.
  Display         Full screen, or in a desktop Window
  Sound           Original, Arcade or Off (see below)
  Arcade voices   (Arcade) One, as the arcade machine: one sound at a
                  time, the more important one winning (the
                  standard); or Many, sounds playing over each other
  Volume          20% to 100%, scaled by the desktop's own volume
                  (!Configure, Sound)
  Controls        Mouse, Keyboard or Gamepad. With Keyboard the mouse
                  doesn't lean the ship and the pointer isn't held.
  Mouse speed     how far a move of the mouse leans the ship, 50% to
                  300%
  Difficulty      (Invasion) Easy (five ships, fewer and slower
                  aliens), Normal, Hard (two ships, more aliens, and
                  some virus is left between waves) or Zarch: the
                  authentic rules, with no hover, no pickups, no free
                  missiles on the pad and a score that can go below 0
  Game speed      Original (the 1987 pace, two thirds of Normal),
                  Normal or Fast
  Particles       how many sparks and specks of smoke there can be:
                  Few (484, as the original), Normal or Lots. It
                  doesn't change the game, only how it looks.
  Shadows         On or Off: black shadows under everything that flies
  Stars and fog   (Enhanced) the stars, and haze in the distance.
                  As in Zarch, fly high and the sky turns black
                  and stars appear all round you. (In the Classic
                  look there are stars in Invasion only.)
  Browser         In page, or Full screen (the whole computer screen)

The two sound sets:

  Original   in the spirit of Zarch on the Archimedes: grainy 8-bit
             samples, a roaring hiss for the engine, crunchy
             explosions, the bombers' whoosh and the pests' twitter.
             (Lander itself had no sound.)
  Arcade     the sound board of the Williams arcade machines of the
             early 1980s, Defender (1981) and Robotron: 2084 (1982),
             recreated: a program that works the way the board's own
             did, so the laser, explosions, smart bomb, extra life,
             wave jingles and high score tune sound as they did in
             the arcade.

The arcade board could play only one sound at a time: a new sound cut
off the one playing, and the game let a sound through only if it
mattered at least as much as the one playing (an explosion drowns a
shot, a shot doesn't cut short the start of an explosion). With Arcade
voices at One the Arcade set does the same, and the engine's rumble
drops right down while another sound plays, as the arcade's background
sound did. As in Defender, a low rumble goes on all the time you are
in the air. Choose Many to hear every sound at once, as the Original
set always does.

Neither set uses sample files: every sound is worked out by the program
when it starts (see the files SfxOrig, SfxArcade and Williams).


7) High scores, and where things are kept
-----------------------------------------

There is a table of the ten best games for each game, Invasion and
Lander+: High scores on the title page (Left and Right switch between
them). The tables start with made-up entries to beat.

Lander II keeps your settings and scores in Choices:Lander2, which on
the hard disc is $.!Boot.Choices.Lander2:

  Settings   your settings
  Scores     the high score tables, and the name you typed last

Both are text files (JSON) that you can read in !Edit. Delete Scores
to start the tables again, or Settings to go back to the standard
settings; a damaged file is ignored.


8) Reading and changing the program
-----------------------------------

All of Lander II is written in JavaScript, and every part of it is in
the application directory, with notes for readers at the top of each
file. To read it, choose View source on the title page or the icon bar
menu: !JsEdit opens the directory in a directory view, and you can
double-click a file to read it. From the Filer, Shift-double-click
!Lander2 to open the directory, then Shift-double-click a file to open
it in !JsEdit (a plain double-click would try to run it).

The files, and what each one does:

  !RunImage   where it starts: the icon bar icon, the screen or window,
              and the main loop, which moves the game on 50 times a
              second and draws each picture
  World       the rules of the game, one 1/50 second step at a time
  Player      how the ship flies (Braben's flight model)
  Enemies     the aliens, their missiles and bombs, and the pickups
  Waves       the difficulty settings and what each wave holds
  Virus       how the infection spreads
  Particles   exhaust, bullets, smoke and sparks
  Terrain     the shape of the land, made from sine waves
  Maths       small helpers used by everything else
  Models      the 3D shape of everything, as lists of triangles
  Render      draws the 3D world, a pixel at a time
  Raster      fills the triangles
  Surface     the block of pixels the game draws into
  Sound       plays the sound effects
  SfxKit      a small synthesiser that makes them
  SfxOrig     the Original sound set
  SfxArcade   the Arcade sound set
  Williams    the Williams arcade sound board, recreated
  Channel     the Arcade set's one voice: which sound gets heard
  Input       the mouse, keys and gamepad
  Autopilot   flies the demonstration game
  Font        text in the RISC OS system font
  Hud         the score panel, gauges and scanner
  Menu        the menus on the title and pause screens
  Screens     the title, high scores, How to play and Settings pages
  Play        a game in progress, the pause menu and the tally
  Settings    the settings, and where they are saved
  Scores      the high score tables

Changing it

Before you change anything, make a copy to work on: drag !Lander2 from
the Diversions directory into a directory of your own (on the hard
disc, say), and change the copy. The original stays as it was, and
you can always compare the two. (Both share the same settings and
scores.)

Here are two small changes to try. In your copy, open Waves in
!JsEdit. Near the top, the line after "normal: {" begins

    lives: 3, missiles: 3, smartBombs: 1,

Change the 3 after "lives:" to 9 and save the file (the Save button on
the toolbar). Or open
Player and find the line

    export const THRUST = 1 / 64;

and make the engine stronger with 1 / 40 (or weaker with 1 / 90).

To try the change, quit Lander II if it is running (Quit on its icon
bar menu), then double-click your copy of !Lander2: the program is
read afresh each time it starts. If you make a mistake, an error box
says which file and line it is in: open that file, put it right and
try again.


9) Credits
----------

Lander is (C) D. J. Braben 1987. Lander II follows the original's
landscape, flight, objects and particles closely, from Mark Moxon's
fully documented commentary on Lander's source code at

  https://lander.bbcelite.com

His deep dive "Hacking the landscape" there, about showing much more of
Lander's landscape at once, inspired Lander II's bigger view. The
aliens and the rules of Invasion are after Zarch (1987) and its later
version Virus, both also by David Braben.

The Arcade sounds recreate the sound board of Defender and Robotron:
2084, both (C) Williams Electronics. The board's sound program, which
Lander II's Williams file follows, was written by Sam Dicker.
